VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: R3
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Claim under test: "a payment the user resolved as a duplicate can no longer
be counted twice in any account balance, low-balance warning, or funding
projection."

REFUTED for the funding-projection clause. Balance and low-balance-warning
clauses hold.

## What the worker actually changed (confirmed via `git diff`)

  internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go:114
    - buildAccountsCard(store, data.Transactions, ...)
    + buildAccountsCard(store, data.Active().Transactions, ...)

  internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/accounts.go:87, :235
    - txns = ts.Transactions
    + txns = ts.Active().Transactions

Both edits are real and correctly filter the BALANCE argument at both named
call sites. `TestGetAccountsCountsSuppressedDuplicateOnce`,
`TestGetBalanceProjectionCountsSuppressedDuplicateOnce`, and
`TestAccountsCard_SuppressedDuplicateCountedOnce` are DISCRIMINATING tests:
I reverted the two-file diff (`git stash push` on just those two files) and
re-ran them — all three FAIL against the pre-fix code with exactly the
double-counted numbers the test comments predict ($400 vs $1300, wrongly
tripped low_balance/crossing/top-up). I then restored the fix (`git stash
pop`) and confirmed `go build ./...` is clean. This part of the claim is not
a rubber-stamp; the tests genuinely pin the fix.

## The defect: the dashboard's recurring/projection input is still raw

`internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go:114` builds
`detectRecurringForDashboard(data, asOf)` from the RAW `data` (not
`data.Active()`), and `detectRecurringForDashboard` (accounts_card.go:241)
passes that raw set straight into `insights.DetectRecurringAt` with no
suppression filtering of its own. This is the twin call site the task brief
did not name (the brief only named the balance argument, `BalanceAt`'s
`txs`), and it was left unfixed. The MCP equivalent
(`recurringForProjection`, accounts.go:276-284) DOES call `.Active()` before
`insights.DetectRecurring`, so the two paths that are supposed to agree now
disagree — this is exactly the "fixed at one call site, not its twin" shape
the dispatch predicted.

I built a fixture (a Go test placed temporarily in
internal/handlers/dashboard/, run with `go test`, then removed — see
commands below for reproduction) with 5 genuine monthly $900 occurrences of
"Lucid Motors Payment" plus one resolved-duplicate row (Suppressed=true,
same amount, 1 day after the 3rd occurrence — the same offset shape as the
worker's own near-duplicate fixtures). Calling the exact production
function `detectRecurringForDashboard` on the raw set vs.
`insights.DetectRecurringAt` on `.Active()`:

  raw (current production path):   freq="ongoing", Occurrences=6,
                                    NextExpected=2026-05-25
  active (correct):                freq="monthly", Occurrences=5,
                                    NextExpected=2026-05-31

The unsuppressed duplicate's near-zero interval corrupts the interval
std-dev enough to knock the series out of the strict "monthly" band into
the loose "ongoing" bucket. `accounts.Project`'s `frequencyIntervalDays`
returns `ok=false` for "ongoing" (projection.go:193-208), so the recurring
outflow is SKIPPED ENTIRELY in the projection loop — not double-counted,
undercounted to zero. Feeding both recurring lists into the real
`accounts.Project` (same account, same active balance input, only the
recurring argument differs) produced:

  buggy (raw recurring):    Crossing = zero (no crossing, "healthy")
                             Minimum  = $1000.00
                             SuggestedTopUp = $0.00
  correct (active recurring): Crossing = 2026-05-31
                             Minimum  = $100.00
                             SuggestedTopUp = $400.00

This is a full-severity funding-projection miss: the dashboard would render
"healthy, no top-up needed" when the correct answer is a threshold crossing
requiring a $400 top-up — directly contradicting the acceptance criterion
"...the funding projection...count[s] the debit exactly once" (the R3 brief
requires the projection to be correct in the presence of a resolved
duplicate; it is not, because the duplicate is still feeding the recurring
detector that seeds the projection).

Reproduction (files were created and then deleted after confirming; not
left in the tree):
  - test file: internal/handlers/dashboard/zz_adversarial_r3_test.go
    (TestZZAdversarial_RecurringPathIgnoresSuppression,
     TestZZAdversarial_ProjectionLineDiverges)
  - `go test ./internal/handlers/dashboard/ -run TestZZAdversarial -v`
    reproduces both the classification divergence and the Crossing/Minimum
    divergence deterministically.

## Secondary findings

- Worker's own tests do not exercise this path: both
  `TestGetBalanceProjectionCountsSuppressedDuplicateOnce` and the dashboard
  card test's comments explicitly note "No recurring items are detected" —
  the fixtures were deliberately (or accidentally) built to avoid the one
  code path that is still broken. The acceptance-criteria checklist ("the
  funding projection...count[s] the debit exactly once") is satisfied only
  for the non-recurring case.
- `recurringForProjection` (mcpsvc, correct) double-filters
  (`.Active()` on an already-`.Active()`-filtered `txns` argument at some
  call sites) — harmless, not a defect, just redundant.
- Grep for all `accounts.BalanceAt(`, `accounts.Freshness(`,
  `accounts.Project(` call sites found exactly the ones named in the brief
  plus the recurring seam; no other unfixed raw-set caller exists for
  BalanceAt/Freshness/Project themselves. The gap is specifically the
  recurring-detection input, which is upstream of Project's `recurring`
  parameter and outside the brief's named locations.
- Explorer test (`TestExplorerListsBothSidesOfAResolvedDuplicate`) does
  discriminate: it asserts the suppressed row's description text is present
  in the rendered body, which would fail if explorer accidentally switched
  to `.Active()`. This criterion is legitimately met and explorer.go was
  not touched by this diff (correctly out of scope per the brief).

## Verdict rationale

The claim as stated ("...or funding projection") is false as shipped. Two
of three balance-adjacent guarantees hold (balance, low-balance warning);
the funding-projection guarantee does not hold whenever the resolved
duplicate sits inside a series the recurring engine would otherwise call
monthly/weekly/etc. Per the "default to FAIL when ambiguous" instruction,
and given this is not ambiguous but reproduced deterministically, I FAIL
this attempt.
